{"custom_id":"s-ap_physics_c_electricity_and_magnetism-m06-k1-q1","ok":true,"model":"claude-opus-5-5","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgEDTmf811Cmzfc8XnX2i","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Outside the wire the field is B = μ0·I/(2πr). Solving for the current gives I = 2πrB/μ0.\n\nAt the surface, r = 4 mm and B = 2000 µT:\nI = 2π(0.004 m)(2.0×10⁻³ T) / (4π×10⁻⁷ T·m/A) = 40 A.\n\nCheck with a point farther out: at r = 8 mm, B = 1000 µT. This gives 0.008 × 0.001 / (2×10⁻⁷) = 40 A, which agrees."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FWZXEAk97mLhkc5iAgASar","name":"submit_solution","input":{"answers":[{"item":"1","choice_index":0,"final_answer":"40.0 A","brief_reasoning":"I = 2πrB/μ0 = 2π(0.004 m)(2.0e-3 T)/(4π×10^-7 T·m/A) = 40 A. The point r = 8 mm, B = 1000 µT gives the same result.","ambiguous_or_flawed":false}]},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1550,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":568,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":363,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"usage":{"input_tokens":1550,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":568,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":363,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"cost":0.0136872}
{"custom_id":"s-ap_physics_c_electricity_and_magnetism-m01-k1-q2","ok":true,"model":"claude-opus-5-5","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgEDTP16rucx3QLkhkq2Y","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"**Answer: (A) 4.90 m/s**\n\nThe particle has only electric potential energy and kinetic energy, so energy is conserved.\n\n1. From the graph, V = 50 V at x = 1 m and V = 20 V at x = 3 m. The potential drops by 30 V.\n2. The kinetic energy gained is q(V₁ − V₃) = (2 × 10⁻⁶ C)(30 V) = 6.0 × 10⁻⁵ J.\n3. The mass is 5 mg = 5 × 10⁻⁶ kg.\n4. Set ½mv² = 6.0 × 10⁻⁵ J. Then v² = 2(6.0 × 10⁻⁵)/(5 × 10⁻⁶) = 24 m²/s², so v = √24 ≈ 4.90 m/s.\n\nOne possible weakness: x = 1 m is a peak in the potential, so the force there isn't defined. Just to the left the force on a positive charge points left, and just to the right it points right. That means the release point alone doesn't decide which way the particle goes. The stem says the particle \"reaches x = 3 m,\" which assumes it moves right, so I didn't mark the item as flawed. If it moves right, it does reach x = 3 m."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RjaU6276mSWUGbrWwt7fKA","name":"submit_solution","input":{"answers":[{"item":"1","choice_index":0,"final_answer":"4.90 m/s","brief_reasoning":"ΔKE = qΔV = 2e-6*(50-20)=6e-5 J; v=sqrt(2*6e-5/5e-6)=sqrt(24)=4.90 m/s","ambiguous_or_flawed":false,"flaw_note":"Minor: x=1 is a potential maximum (kink), so the initial force direction isn't defined. The stem assumes the particle reaches x=3."}]},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":459,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":568,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1006,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":366},"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"usage":{"input_tokens":459,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":568,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1006,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":366},"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"cost":0.0221832}
{"custom_id":"s-ap_physics_c_electricity_and_magnetism-m06-k0-q2","ok":true,"model":"claude-opus-5-5","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgEDTjpEMnhx7Sdu8c93C","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"At R = 2 mm the field is B = 1000 µT. For a long wire, B = μ0·I/(2πR), so the total current is:\n\nI = 2πR·B/μ0 = (2π × 0.002 m × 1×10⁻³ T) / (4π×10⁻⁷ T·m/A) = 10 A.\n\nBecause the current is spread evenly over the cross-section, the current inside radius R/2 is the total times the area fraction (1/2)² = 1/4. That gives 10 A × 1/4 = 2.5 A.\n\nCheck with Ampère's law directly: at r = 1 mm the graph shows B = 500 µT, so I_enc = (2π × 0.001 m × 5×10⁻⁴ T) / (4π×10⁻⁷ T·m/A) = 2.5 A. This matches."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Mv7eLrWUMRuw5n248uGugE","name":"submit_solution","input":{"answers":[{"item":"1","brief_reasoning":"B(R)=1000µT at R=2mm gives I=10 A. The enclosed current scales as r², so (1/2)² × 10 A = 2.5 A. Ampère's law at r=1mm (B=500µT) gives the same 2.5 A.","ambiguous_or_flawed":false,"choice_index":3,"final_answer":"2.50 A"}]},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1519,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":568,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":461,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"usage":{"input_tokens":1519,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":568,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":461,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"cost":0.015523200000000001}
